  and not (depth = 'research' and level = 'advanced' and format = 'read');

with q as (
  select $astroIce_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "In what sense are Uranus and Neptune a genuinely different class of planet from Jupiter and Saturn, and how does formation timing explain it?", "options": ["They are simply smaller versions of Jupiter, made of the same hydrogen-dominated recipe scaled down to a lower mass, differing only in size and temperature.", "They are mostly hydrogen but happen to be denser because they formed nearer the Sun where the disk was hotter and more compressed.", "They are 'ice giants' whose bulk is heavier volatiles (water, ammonia, methane) plus rock, with only a 10-20 percent hydrogen-helium wrapping, because their ten-Earth-mass cores reached the runaway-accretion threshold only as the gas disk was already dissipating and so missed the chance to gorge on hydrogen.", "They are failed stars whose cores never grew large enough to fuse hydrogen, leaving a rock ball with no atmosphere at all."], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "The densities give it away: Jupiter (1,326 kg/m3) and Saturn (687) sit near the density of water because they are mostly hydrogen, while Uranus (1,270) and Neptune (1,638) are about half again denser and so cannot be hydrogen-dominated. Reading outward, an ice giant is a rock-iron core, a deep hot 'icy' fluid mantle (water-ammonia-methane) that is the majority of the mass, and only a thin 10-20 percent hydrogen-helium envelope. This is the exact inversion of Jupiter, which is gas wrapped around a whisper of heavy elements. The cause is timing: beyond the snow line a core must reach about ten Earth masses before the gas disk evaporates (a few million years) to capture the disk and become a Jupiter. At 20 and 30 AU the disk was thinner (less solid material per unit area) and orbits were slower (a Neptune 'year' is 165 of ours), so the cores crept to the threshold only as the gas was already blowing away. They grabbed the 10-20 percent of hydrogen still within reach and no more: a giant planet that almost, but not quite, got the gas."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What does 'ice' actually mean when an astronomer says Uranus and Neptune are made of ice?", "options": ["It names a chemical class and condensation temperature, not a physical state: water, ammonia, and methane condense at intermediate temperatures and were delivered as ice grains in the cold disk, but inside the planet they are a searing fluid at thousands of kelvin and millions of atmospheres.", "It means the interiors are literally frozen solid, cold crystalline water like the cubes in a glass, kept below freezing by the feeble distant Sun.", "It refers to a thin frozen crust of surface ice floating on a liquid-hydrogen ocean, the way sea ice floats on Earth's oceans.", "It is a loose nickname for any light-coloured gas, and carries no real compositional meaning."], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "In planet-formation vocabulary matter sorts into three bins by how easily it vaporises: gas (hydrogen and helium, condensing only near absolute zero), rock and metal (silicates and iron, condensing above ~1,000 K), and ices (water, ammonia, methane, condensing at a few hundred kelvin and below, out past the snow line). 'Ice' therefore records a chemical class and a condensation temperature, not a state. The label says only that these molecules were delivered to the growing planet as ice grains in the cold disk. Once buried and compressed, the 'ice' inside Uranus is neither cold nor solid; it is a hot, dense, electrically conducting fluid at thousands of kelvin and millions of atmospheres. So there is no contradiction in a searing-hot 'ice' mantle: chemistry, not phase, is what the word tracks."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why do Uranus and Neptune appear blue-green, and why is Neptune measurably bluer despite the two having comparable methane?", "options": ["The colour comes from Rayleigh scattering off a deep hydrogen ocean, and Neptune is bluer because its ocean is deeper.", "Neptune is bluer because it simply contains far more methane than Uranus, so it absorbs more red light.", "The blue is sunlight reflecting off vast fields of surface water ice, and Neptune's ice is fresher and therefore bluer.", "Methane absorbs red light and lets bluer wavelengths scatter back, giving both planets their blue-green cast; the extra blue of Neptune is not chemistry but meteorology, because vigorous mixing keeps its whitish haze layer thin while sluggish Uranus lets that haze pile up and dilute the blue."], "answerIndex": 3, "explanation": "The first-order cause is methane, CH4, present at a few percent in the upper atmosphere (far more than on Jupiter or Saturn). Methane has a strong absorption band in the red, so it drinks in the long wavelengths of incoming and outgoing sunlight and lets the shorter, bluer wavelengths scatter back, returning a blue-green disc. You can even watch the colour deepen where light travels a longer path through more methane, which is why deeper cloud decks and limbs look bluer. But methane alone predicts that Uranus and Neptune, with comparable methane, should be nearly the same colour, and they are not: Neptune is visibly bluer. The current best answer is a haze difference. Both worlds carry a pale, sunlight-scattering haze; on Uranus sluggish vertical mixing lets it thicken and wash the planet toward grey-green, while Neptune's more vigorous churning keeps the haze thin so the underlying methane-blue shows through more purely. The colour difference is meteorology, not chemistry, and the received 'more methane' explanation is simply wrong."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "How do the internal heat budgets of Uranus and Neptune compare, and why does the colder, better-lit planet end up calmer?", "options": ["Neptune radiates about 2.6 times the sunlight it absorbs while Uranus radiates only about 1.1 times (an excess consistent with zero); Neptune's strong internal heat drives its violent weather from below, whereas Uranus, with a dead or sealed furnace, has almost nothing but feeble sunlight to stir it and so lies calm.", "Both planets radiate roughly 2.6 times what they absorb, so their weather is equally violent and any difference is just observational error.", "Uranus radiates far more internal heat than Neptune, which is why the closer, warmer planet has the wilder storms, exactly as intuition predicts.", "Neither planet has measurable internal heat; all their weather is powered by sunlight, so the closer planet Uranus is naturally the stormier one."], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Neptune absorbs about 2.0x10^15 W of sunlight but emits about 5.3x10^15 W, a ratio of 2.6, so it generates over three petawatts of its own heat, right in line with the gas giants and their slowly-cooling interiors. Uranus absorbs about 5.2x10^15 W and emits about 5.6x10^15 W, a ratio of only 1.1 that is, within error, consistent with zero internal flux. Alone among the giant planets Uranus appears not to be leaking the heat of its own formation, one of the sharpest unsolved problems in planetary science. The meteorological twist inverts intuition: Neptune sits farther out (30 AU vs 19) and gets less than half the sunlight per unit area, only about 1/900 of Earth's, yet has the solar system's most violent weather, while Uranus in 1986 was nearly featureless. Weather is driven by the energy flux passing through the atmosphere, and for these worlds that flux comes overwhelmingly from below. Neptune's furnace pumps energy up to drive convection and winds; Uranus's dead or sealed interior leaves only weak sunlight to stir it. The light was never the point."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why are the magnetic fields of Uranus and Neptune so tilted, offset, and multipolar, unlike the tidy near-axial dipoles of Earth, Jupiter, and Saturn?", "options": ["Their fields are actually clean dipoles, and the apparent tilt is an artefact of Voyager 2 measuring during a magnetic pole reversal in progress.", "Their dynamos run in a comparatively thin convecting shell of ionised water and ammonia in the outer icy mantle, above a deeper stably-stratified region; a shell dynamo does not enforce alignment with the spin axis and naturally produces strongly tilted, offset, multipolar fields.", "The tilt comes from the 98-degree obliquity of Uranus physically dragging the field sideways, and Neptune's field is tilted by tidal pull from its large moon Triton.", "Both planets lack any internal dynamo, so their fields are frozen-in relics of the solar wind, which points in random directions."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Every field met before the ice giants was, to first order, a bar magnet near the centre roughly aligned with the spin axis: Earth tilted 11 degrees, Jupiter and Saturn aligned to within a few degrees. That near-alignment falls out when a dynamo runs in a deep, thick, rotating, convecting region, because rotation organises the flow into axis-aligned columns. Voyager 2 found the opposite at Uranus (tilt about 59 degrees, dipole centre offset roughly a third of a radius) and Neptune (tilt about 47 degrees, offset more than half a radius), and both are strongly multipolar with quadrupole and higher components comparable to the dipole. The explanation ties back to composition: the ice giants have no deep iron core or metallic-hydrogen ocean. Their conducting region is the icy mantle, and only a comparatively thin outer shell of it convects, sitting above a deeper, likely stratified layer that does not participate. A dynamo in a thin shell rather than a deep sphere produces exactly the tilted, offset, multipolar geometry Voyager saw, because the flow no longer enforces axial alignment. A field that points the wrong way is thus a measurement telling you the conducting layer is shallow and shell-like: real information about a mantle no instrument has touched."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Explain the superionic phase proposed for the water in an ice-giant mantle: what is mobile and what is fixed, why it is described as 'a solid that flows,' why it matters for generating a magnetic field, and how its discovery illustrates a 'loop' between planetary science and condensed-matter physics.", "rubric": "A strong answer defines superionic water precisely: at the pressures and temperatures of an ice-giant mantle (of order millions of atmospheres and several thousand kelvin), the OXYGEN atoms remain locked in a rigid crystal lattice while the HYDROGEN nuclei (protons) break free and stream through that lattice like a liquid (or like electrons in a metal). It is therefore simultaneously a genuine SOLID (fixed oxygen scaffold that holds a shape and has a lattice) and a liquid-like CONDUCTOR (mobile charged protons) - hence 'a solid that flows/conducts like a liquid.' It should explain WHY this matters for a dynamo: a magnetic field requires a good electrical conductor, and ordinary liquid water conducts poorly; because the mobile species are charged protons, superionic water is a superb IONIC conductor, precisely the property a dynamo needs, so a mantle largely of superionic water (and its ammonia analogue) is electrically conducting through much of its volume and convection in its fluid part can drive the tilted shell dynamos. Bonus: superionic ice may be one of the more common states of water in the universe given how abundant ice-giant-mass planets are, even though none exists naturally on Earth. Finally it should describe the LOOP: the phase was first a pure prediction of computer simulations of water under extreme compression, motivated by the demands of ice-giant interior models; it was then MADE in the laboratory by shock-compressing water with high-power lasers to the required pressures and temperatures for fleeting instants, detecting its density, conductivity, and crystalline oxygen lattice; and the lab result feeds back to constrain the planet models. It should cite the explicit PARALLEL with metallic hydrogen from the gas-giant story - predicted for Jupiter's interior and then chased in compression labs - and articulate the general point: we study distant planet interiors to motivate exotic condensed-matter experiments, and we run the experiments to understand the planets."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Uranus's 98-degree tilt and its 42-year seasons, and the fact that Voyager 2 flew past each ice giant exactly once, are both central to the chapter's closing argument. Explain the geometry that gives Uranus its 42-year polar day and night, note what proposed event may connect the tilt to Uranus's missing internal heat, and then make the case for why a flagship orbiter mission to the ice giants is a scientific priority.", "rubric": "A strong answer has three parts. (1) THE TILT AND SEASONS: Uranus's rotation axis is tipped about 98 degrees, past horizontal, so it spins essentially lying down with its axis nearly in the ecliptic plane (past 90 degrees the rotation is technically retrograde - the same fact stated two ways). With the axis in the orbital plane, for part of the 84-year orbit the north pole points nearly straight at the Sun and sits in continuous daylight while the south pole is in continuous darkness; half an orbit later the situation reverses. Because each pole faces sunward for roughly half the orbit, each endures about 84/2 = 42 years of unbroken sunlight then 42 years of unbroken night (a single polar 'day' of 84 years). Only near the equinoxes (as in 2007) does the Sun cross the equator and both hemispheres briefly get an ordinary rapid day-night cycle. When Voyager 2 arrived in 1986 the south pole pointed almost straight at the Sun. (2) TILT-HEAT CONNECTION: the leading explanation for the tilt is catastrophe - one or more giant impacts by bodies of one to several Earth masses, which also plausibly explains why the regular moons and rings lie in the same tipped equatorial plane (debris re-accreted about the new equator). The same impact may connect to the dead-furnace puzzle: it could have scrambled or shut down interior convection and reset the thermal state (the 'heat really is missing' option), as opposed to the alternative that the heat is trapped behind a stably-stratified insulating layer (the 'thermos' option). The two puzzles - the tilt and the missing heat - may be one event seen from two angles. (3) THE MISSION CASE: Voyager 2 flew past Uranus in January 1986 and Neptune in August 1989, days each, and remains the ONLY in-situ data for both; no orbiter has ever been sent, so nearly every statement is hedged because the data are thin and 40+ years old. The case rests on two legs. Parochial: the ice giants are the least-explored class of planet, so return per dollar is enormous; a Uranus orbiter with an atmospheric probe (Uranus favoured for trajectory/launch reasons) has repeatedly topped the decadal survey, and could directly measure internal heat flow to settle the furnace puzzle, map the multipolar field over time, sound the deep atmosphere, and probe the interior with gravity as Juno did at Jupiter. Universal: exoplanet surveys find the single most common size class is between Earth and Neptune - the ice-giant regime - so understanding Uranus and Neptune means understanding the default outcome of planet formation across the galaxy, which cannot be done from a 1980s fly-by."}]$astroIce_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/solar-system/giant-planets/ice-giants', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
